<p>That said, it does lack a certain "zing" as currently written. The part about throwing off the actual dimensions of objects currently feels a bit incongruous with the rest of the article - I'd suggest maybe expanding on that, and explaining why that aspect of the effect doesn't immediately cause fatal glitches in human bodies (maybe something that's already assembled is treated as a single object?). Also, adding some sort of effect of long-term exposure could punch it up a bit. I can just imagine someone removing the locket for testing, and finding that the legs of the table it had been resting on have been left permanently uneven. There's also the part about trying to measure affected measurements producing errors, regardless of location - is that an isolated effect, or is an area around the attempt temporarily caught in the effect?</p>

							</div>

								
					
